{
  "content": "## Review
- Blocker: none. Quick-bubble expiry/config, per-instance wander-pause persistence, and roam mode handoff are wired through the current instance and pass the targeted regression suite.
- P2 - `windows/src/i18n.ts:957`, `windows/src/settings.ts:1280`, `windows/src/settings.ts:1297`, `windows/src/settings.ts:1352`, `windows/src/pet-window.ts:200`: new visible strings fall back to English for supported non-English languages. `applyStatic()` resolves `Create` and `Icon`, but no exact keys exist in any of the `vi`, `zh`, or `zh-TW` dictionaries; `Stats` is also absent from `zh-TW`. The runtime level-up bubble likewise resolves the missing `Level up` key. As `t()` returns the source key when a translation is absent, these are user-visible localization regressions.
- P2 - `windows/src/styles.css:158-169`, `windows/src/styles.css:1688-1707`: the new infinite `aura-breathe` animation on the Settings pet stage is not disabled by either the in-app `body.reduce-motion` selector or `prefers-reduced-motion`. This contradicts the Reduce motion setting's stated behavior and keeps repainting the Settings WebView while the setting is enabled.
- P3 - `windows/src/settings-layout.test.ts:40-52`, `windows/src/performance-contract.test.ts:13-103`: tests cover the existence of quick-duration/wander-pause controls and selected runtime performance contracts, but no test validates that strings newly passed through `t()` exist for all supported dictionaries or that every infinite animation is disabled by the in-app/OS reduced-motion paths. The two P2 regressions therefore pass the suite.

- Correct: `windows/src/quick-bubble.ts:34-64` cancels superseded timers, ignores stale generations, and re-arms an early callback; `windows/src/pet-window.ts:157-164,221-232` clears the render cache on both expiry callback and visible-to-normal transition. `windows/src/quick-bubble.test.ts:45-73` and `windows/src/pet-window-quick-bubble.test.ts:6-15` cover these contracts.
- Correct: `windows/src/pets.ts:52-63,171-187,209-220` normalizes and persists the pause range per instance, while `windows/src/pet-window.ts:50-67` injects that instance's config into roaming. `windows/src/roam/modes.ts:92-102,394-416` consumes it when a wander target is reached, and `windows/src/roam/modes.ts:40-46` clears stale target/rest state on a mode transition.
- Correct: `windows/src/roam/engine.ts:94-100,138-179,259-263` uses a 200 ms idle tick after non-moving states, while preserving the 30 ms tick for movement and interaction. The focused tests passed.

